WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.001 line:0%
-Welcome back, everybody.

00:00:02.001 --> 00:00:03.669 line:0%
I'm here with the one-and-only
David Schwimmer.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:05.238 line:0%
Thank you very much
for doing our show.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.140 line:0%
I really appreciate it, David.
-No problem. Yeah.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.676 line:0%
-"Intelligence" -- super-funny
show.

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:12.144 line:0%
Already got picked up
for Season 2.

00:00:12.144 --> 00:00:13.947 line:0%
-Yeah.
-So congrats on that.

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:15.648 line:0%
It's on Peacock, which I was
playing around with

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:18.617 line:0%
this weekend, because,
obviously, it's our network,

00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:20.853 line:0%
and I was -- I just wanted to
see what it was all about.

00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:21.987 line:0%
Not bad.
-Yeah.

00:00:21.987 --> 00:00:23.255 line:0%
-Actually good stuff.

00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:24.824 line:0%
I got into it and, like,

00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:27.026 line:0%
I was already watching your show
in two minutes.

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:28.261 line:0%
It was great.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.562 line:0%
-Oh, good. Thanks.
Thank you.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:33.399 line:0%
-I like your
physical comedy in this.

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:36.069 line:0%
There's some nods to, like, a --

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:39.172 line:0%
like Peter Sellers-ish
sometimes.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.407 line:0%
There's one where you run
and slide down the --

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:44.610 line:0%
and just fall off the desk.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.312 line:0%
-Yeah.
I mean, I love physical comedy.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:49.115 line:0%
You know, I grew up -- I don't
know who your heroes were

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.150 line:0%
when you were growing up,
but, like, as a kid,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.486 line:0%
I was addicted to Monty Python.

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:57.657 line:0%
You know, I would watch
and study those guys

00:00:57.657 --> 00:01:00.493 line:0%
as much as I could
and I'd mimic.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:03.562 line:0%
I'd just try to do every single
silly walk there was.

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:08.234 line:0%
And, yeah, so, I mean, I just --
I was hooked early on.

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:12.405 line:0%
-Did you entertain
at parties for your parents?

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:15.774 line:0%
-Not at parties, but --
Well, probably when my parents

00:01:15.774 --> 00:01:18.611 line:0%
had folks over, company,
I would probably be

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:21.147 line:0%
inappropriately jumping in
and hurling myself

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:22.582 line:0%
against furniture.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.950 line:0%
No, yeah, I mean that's how --

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.853 line:0%
I just loved
making my family laugh --

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:28.954 line:0%
my parents and my sister.

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:30.857 line:0%
So I'd just be bouncing off
the walls

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:34.593 line:0%
and hurling myself
over couches and --

00:01:34.593 --> 00:01:36.762 line:0%
Anyway, when you're young,
you know, you don't get hurt.

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:38.497 line:0%
It's only much older
that you realize,

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:42.101 line:0%
"Oh, I'd better develop a
technique, because --"

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:43.803 line:0%
-Yeah, when you're a kid,
there's no technique.

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.404 line:0%
You go for it.

00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:47.940 line:0%
I pretended I was Chevy Chase
like every single day.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.110 line:0%
I remember once, I fell down the
stairs with paper plates

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.511 line:0%
and I was like, "What..."

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:54.013 line:0%
I could never even --"
And I'd be like,

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.181 line:0%
"Is there a stunt thing?

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:56.549 line:0%
Is there a pillow
just to make sure it's safe?

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:57.750 line:0%
And should I wear a helmet?"

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.785 line:0%
I could never get away
with it now.

00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:02.655 line:0%
Do you want to set up
who you play on the show?

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:07.359 line:0%
-Sure. So, "Intelligence" --
it's a workplace comedy,

00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:11.764 line:0%
but it's set in London --
well, outside London --

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:17.036 line:0%
in Britain's version of the NSA,
which is called GCHQ.

00:02:17.036 --> 00:02:21.473 line:0%
And they're really dealing with
national security,

00:02:21.473 --> 00:02:24.076 line:0%
cyberterrorism, and cybercrime.

00:02:24.076 --> 00:02:30.617 line:0%
And I play this kind of pompous,
arrogant alpha-male American

00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:32.852 line:0%
who's shipped over from the NSA.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:37.156 line:0%
I'm an NSA agent who's shipped
over to work with the Brits

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:39.158 line:0%
on a new program
where we combine

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:42.195 line:0%
our intelligence
and our resources.

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:45.398 line:0%
And I basically assume
that I'm in control

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.033 line:0%
and I'm over here to run things,

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:52.004 line:0%
when, in fact, we learn
that I've been demoted

00:02:52.004 --> 00:02:56.809 line:0%
and the NSA is trying to, like,
get me out of the spotlight

00:02:56.809 --> 00:02:59.345 line:0%
because I've been --
My activities

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:04.217 line:0%
have been perhaps
less than honorable at the NSA.

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:06.485 line:0%
We find out a lot about,
you know,

00:03:06.485 --> 00:03:08.587 line:0%
my character
as the series progresses.

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:11.657 line:0%
But, basically,
it's a workplace ensemble comedy

00:03:11.657 --> 00:03:15.728 line:0%
set in the high-stakes world
of national intelligence.

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:19.532 line:0%
-Did anyone from the NSA
reach out to you at all or...

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:25.504 line:0%
-I'm assuming they're reading
all of my e-mail and --

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:26.539 line:0%
-They were watching you.

00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:27.874 line:0%
Yeah, even before this
interview aired,

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:29.075 line:0%
they're like, "Great interview."

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:33.812 line:0%
-I got a random e-mail
with notes on Season 3

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:36.916 line:0%
and -- No.

00:03:36.916 --> 00:03:39.218 line:0%
Yeah, no,
we haven't spoken to the NSA.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:41.621 line:0%
Although Nick,
the creator of the show

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:44.657 line:0%
and my co-star,
he did meet with GCHQ,

00:03:44.657 --> 00:03:47.092 line:0%
some reps from GCHQ,
who wanted to make sure

00:03:47.092 --> 00:03:49.862 line:0%
we weren't kind of, as they say,
"taking the piss."

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.030 line:0%
-Yeah.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:53.866 line:0%
-And they were --
I think they were reassured

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:56.336 line:0%
that we were going to
make them look good,

00:03:56.336 --> 00:03:58.837 line:0%
even though we're a bunch of

00:03:58.837 --> 00:04:01.808 line:0%
kind of misfits
and incompetents.

00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:03.809 line:0%
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:05.711 line:0%
Here's David Schwimmer in
"Intelligence."

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:07.012 line:0%
Take a look at this.

00:04:07.012 --> 00:04:09.248
-One of the things I've
started to realize is that

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:13.152
it's much easier doing this
when you have color photos.

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:15.655
-Gosh, that's a very diverse
bunch, isn't it?

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:16.723
-Thank you.

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:18.123
-I'm worried
it's a bit too diverse.

00:04:18.123 --> 00:04:21.593
-Okay, so, what I've done here
is assimilate all the data

00:04:21.593 --> 00:04:24.897
from the program,
along with Tuva's trace matches

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.298
and combine that
with an algorithm

00:04:26.298 --> 00:04:28.901
that basically mimics
my own gut instincts.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.170
Anyway, taking all of that
into account,

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.240
these are our top-six suspects.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.110
-You know two of these
are the same person?

00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:41.914
-Yeah. It was a test.
You passed.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.016 line:0%
-My thanks to David Schwimmer.

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:47.986 line:0%
His show, "Intelligence,"
is streaming now on Peacock.

00:04:47.986 --> 00:04:49.455 line:0%
David, I really appreciate
you coming on.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.190 line:0%
Really, thank so much, bud.
-Thank you, man.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:52.391 line:0%
Thanks for having me.
Stay safe.

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:53.760 line:0%
-I hope to see you in person.
Yeah, you too.

